1
00:00:00,043 --> 00:00:01,044
GridBot Builder.

2
00:00:01,634 --> 00:00:02,784
GridBot Blueprint.

3
00:00:03,349 --> 00:00:04,113
Lesson two.

4
00:00:04,633 --> 00:00:06,141
What you are going to need.

5
00:00:06,952 --> 00:00:08,098
Education only.

6
00:00:08,657 --> 00:00:09,971
Not financial advice.

7
00:00:10,491 --> 00:00:12,314
This is general information

8
00:00:12,314 --> 00:00:14,746
and doesn't consider your objectives,

9
00:00:14,746 --> 00:00:16,646
financial situation or needs.

10
00:00:17,166 --> 00:00:18,930
Crypto is high risk; you could

11
00:00:18,930 --> 00:00:20,621
lose all the money you put in.

12
00:00:21,141 --> 00:00:23,192
Past performance is not a reliable

13
00:00:23,192 --> 00:00:24,890
indicator of future results.

14
00:00:25,940 --> 00:00:27,399
A daily closing price is

15
00:00:27,399 --> 00:00:28,931
the last price of that day.

16
00:00:29,451 --> 00:00:31,525
The prices are public historical

17
00:00:31,525 --> 00:00:33,302
daily prices, marked Example.

18
00:00:34,502 --> 00:00:36,401
No order is sent from this lesson.

19
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:39,908
Crypto is a digital coin, such as bitcoin.

20
00:00:40,428 --> 00:00:42,562
A range is the top and the bottom,

21
00:00:42,562 --> 00:00:44,942
a gap is the space between two lines,

22
00:00:44,942 --> 00:00:47,241
and the step is how wide that gap is.

23
00:00:47,761 --> 00:00:49,799
A stop is the rule for what you will

24
00:00:49,799 --> 00:00:51,692
do if the price leaves the range.

25
00:00:52,212 --> 00:00:54,111
Like a packing list before a trip,

26
00:00:54,111 --> 00:00:56,011
your page comes before any screen.

27
00:00:56,935 --> 00:00:58,443
By the end you will fill in

28
00:00:58,443 --> 00:01:00,382
a seven-item checklist on paper.

29
00:01:01,582 --> 00:01:03,873
You will keep every secret off that page.

30
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:06,725
You will mark the page Example so

31
00:01:06,725 --> 00:01:08,457
nobody can read it as an order.

32
00:01:09,565 --> 00:01:11,729
The seven items are: a notebook,

33
00:01:11,729 --> 00:01:14,153
three words, range, levels and stop,

34
00:01:14,153 --> 00:01:16,404
the pair, paper or a demo account,

35
00:01:16,404 --> 00:01:18,481
no secrets, a written stop, and

36
00:01:18,481 --> 00:01:20,818
the word Example on every figure.

37
00:01:21,338 --> 00:01:22,719
Each item has a reason.

38
00:01:23,239 --> 00:01:24,580
The notebook is the plan

39
00:01:24,580 --> 00:01:25,697
you can check later.

40
00:01:25,950 --> 00:01:27,738
The three words stop you drawing

41
00:01:27,738 --> 00:01:28,911
a grid with no edges.

42
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:30,789
The pair names what you buy

43
00:01:30,789 --> 00:01:32,018
and what you pay with.

44
00:01:32,392 --> 00:01:33,856
Paper or a demo is learner

45
00:01:33,856 --> 00:01:35,390
plates, or training wheels.

46
00:01:35,910 --> 00:01:38,089
No secrets keeps the keys off the page.

47
00:01:38,582 --> 00:01:40,313
The written stop is the action.

48
00:01:40,606 --> 00:01:42,394
Example marks a teaching number.

49
00:01:42,805 --> 00:01:44,173
For this course the pair

50
00:01:44,173 --> 00:01:45,960
is bitcoin priced in US dollars.

51
00:01:46,471 --> 00:01:48,664
A demo account uses pretend money.

52
00:01:49,184 --> 00:01:50,643
A daily closing price is

53
00:01:50,643 --> 00:01:52,101
the last price of the day.

54
00:01:52,622 --> 00:01:54,074
You are naming that public

55
00:01:54,074 --> 00:01:55,638
series, not choosing an app.

56
00:01:56,450 --> 00:01:58,182
Three words, before any number.

57
00:01:58,587 --> 00:02:01,172
Range is the top line and the bottom line.

58
00:02:01,692 --> 00:02:03,716
An order is an instruction to buy

59
00:02:03,716 --> 00:02:05,665
or sell that can wait for a price.

60
00:02:06,185 --> 00:02:08,552
Levels are the price lines where orders

61
00:02:08,552 --> 00:02:10,619
wait, like the rungs of a ladder, and

62
00:02:10,619 --> 00:02:12,798
a gap is the space between two of them.

63
00:02:13,231 --> 00:02:15,310
A stop is the rule for what you will

64
00:02:15,310 --> 00:02:17,537
do if a daily closing price finishes

65
00:02:17,537 --> 00:02:19,764
outside the range and the next daily

66
00:02:19,764 --> 00:02:21,694
closing price is still outside.

67
00:02:22,214 --> 00:02:24,034
The figures from lesson 1 are

68
00:02:24,034 --> 00:02:25,703
on the card, marked Example.

69
00:02:26,223 --> 00:02:27,899
If you can only say one of the

70
00:02:27,899 --> 00:02:30,133
three words, the page is missing a part.

71
00:02:31,004 --> 00:02:33,126
A wallet is where your crypto is held.

72
00:02:33,443 --> 00:02:35,342
A seed phrase is the list of words

73
00:02:35,342 --> 00:02:36,749
that unlocks that wallet.

74
00:02:37,269 --> 00:02:38,666
A private key is a secret

75
00:02:38,666 --> 00:02:40,133
that can move your crypto.

76
00:02:40,653 --> 00:02:43,366
An API secret is a password that lets

77
00:02:43,366 --> 00:02:46,079
a program place orders on an account,

78
00:02:46,079 --> 00:02:48,611
and sometimes move money out of it.

79
00:02:49,131 --> 00:02:51,943
You never type a seed phrase, a private

80
00:02:51,943 --> 00:02:54,483
key, or an API secret onto this page.

81
00:02:55,003 --> 00:02:56,801
The chart has no balance on it.

82
00:02:57,322 --> 00:02:58,643
A public closing price

83
00:02:58,643 --> 00:02:59,760
is not your account.

84
00:03:00,276 --> 00:03:01,907
If a form asks for a secret,

85
00:03:01,907 --> 00:03:03,693
that form is not this lesson.

86
00:03:04,743 --> 00:03:06,716
You copy the range onto the page,

87
00:03:06,716 --> 00:03:08,159
not every day's prices.

88
00:03:08,679 --> 00:03:10,913
Copy the bottom line and the top

89
00:03:10,913 --> 00:03:13,061
line, with six gaps of about 900.

90
00:03:13,581 --> 00:03:15,575
Every closing price in that window

91
00:03:15,575 --> 00:03:16,951
stayed inside the range.

92
00:03:17,529 --> 00:03:18,931
Mark each figure Example.

93
00:03:19,981 --> 00:03:21,598
Worked check: pair, range,

94
00:03:21,598 --> 00:03:23,052
gaps, stop, and Example.

95
00:03:23,572 --> 00:03:25,787
If the stop line is only a tick with no

96
00:03:25,787 --> 00:03:27,854
sentence, the page fails the check.

97
00:03:28,374 --> 00:03:30,724
The rule has to say: if a daily closing

98
00:03:30,724 --> 00:03:32,917
price finishes outside the range

99
00:03:32,917 --> 00:03:35,032
and the next daily closing price

100
00:03:35,032 --> 00:03:37,382
is still outside, the example is over.

101
00:03:38,432 --> 00:03:40,443
A page is the notebook you can hold.

102
00:03:40,939 --> 00:03:42,900
An exchange is the app or website

103
00:03:42,900 --> 00:03:44,861
where people buy and sell crypto.

104
00:03:45,381 --> 00:03:46,890
The screen is the exchange.

105
00:03:47,320 --> 00:03:48,843
Lesson 1 ended by asking

106
00:03:48,843 --> 00:03:50,137
you to bring the page.

107
00:03:50,657 --> 00:03:52,054
This lesson is that page.

108
00:03:52,569 --> 00:03:54,580
Open the notebook before any screen.

109
00:03:55,491 --> 00:03:57,558
Write today's date on the first line.

110
00:03:57,907 --> 00:03:59,751
Under it, write the word Example.

111
00:04:00,134 --> 00:04:01,613
Example means a real past

112
00:04:01,613 --> 00:04:03,021
price used for practice.

113
00:04:03,541 --> 00:04:05,664
It is not an order and not a forecast.

114
00:04:06,789 --> 00:04:07,962
Write the pair first.

115
00:04:08,366 --> 00:04:09,762
The base is what you buy.

116
00:04:10,263 --> 00:04:11,995
The quote is what you pay with.

117
00:04:12,329 --> 00:04:15,504
For this course the prices are the July

118
00:04:15,504 --> 00:04:18,579
to October example window, the public

119
00:04:18,579 --> 00:04:21,555
bitcoin closing prices from 19 July

120
00:04:21,555 --> 00:04:24,531
to 16 October 2023, so you buy the base,

121
00:04:24,531 --> 00:04:27,309
bitcoin, with the quote, US dollars,

122
00:04:27,309 --> 00:04:29,987
and that row is labelled BTC/USD.

123
00:04:30,507 --> 00:04:32,519
Every other row depends on the pair.

124
00:04:32,741 --> 00:04:34,322
We price in US dollars.

125
00:04:34,842 --> 00:04:36,594
Your exchange may charge fees

126
00:04:36,594 --> 00:04:38,066
in Australian dollars, so

127
00:04:38,066 --> 00:04:39,630
keep each in its own column.

128
00:04:40,128 --> 00:04:42,304
If a fee sum is in Australian

129
00:04:42,304 --> 00:04:44,480
dollars, that sum goes on its

130
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:46,561
own line, labelled BTC/AUD.

131
00:04:47,081 --> 00:04:48,987
Do not put a US dollar price and

132
00:04:48,987 --> 00:04:51,503
an Australian dollar fee on the same row.

133
00:04:52,553 --> 00:04:55,138
Range is the top line and the bottom line.

134
00:04:55,894 --> 00:05:00,376
In the July to October example window

135
00:05:00,873 --> 00:05:04,059
those lines are 24,930 and 30,331.

136
00:05:04,579 --> 00:05:05,976
Both figures are Example.

137
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:08,379
Levels are the lines between them.

138
00:05:08,669 --> 00:05:10,828
Six gaps of about 900 dollars sit

139
00:05:10,828 --> 00:05:12,826
between the top and the bottom.

140
00:05:13,346 --> 00:05:15,202
Write the top and the bottom only

141
00:05:15,202 --> 00:05:17,213
after you have said what a range is.

142
00:05:17,646 --> 00:05:19,773
Then write one gap, and how you got

143
00:05:19,773 --> 00:05:21,505
it: the top minus the bottom,

144
00:05:21,505 --> 00:05:23,395
divided by the number of gaps.

145
00:05:24,445 --> 00:05:25,715
The stop sentence has

146
00:05:25,715 --> 00:05:27,056
to say what you will do.

147
00:05:27,506 --> 00:05:29,313
A bot is a program that places

148
00:05:29,313 --> 00:05:31,723
and cancels the waiting orders for you.

149
00:05:32,243 --> 00:05:34,319
Two daily closing prices outside

150
00:05:34,319 --> 00:05:35,876
the range, through the top

151
00:05:35,876 --> 00:05:37,582
or the bottom, and the second

152
00:05:37,582 --> 00:05:39,361
closing price still outside.

153
00:05:39,881 --> 00:05:41,445
Then you stop the bot, which

154
00:05:41,445 --> 00:05:43,033
cancels the waiting orders,

155
00:05:43,033 --> 00:05:44,933
and you follow the rule you wrote.

156
00:05:45,444 --> 00:05:47,232
Write that stop in one sentence.

157
00:05:48,194 --> 00:05:50,924
A deposit is crypto or Australian dollars

158
00:05:50,924 --> 00:05:53,421
you send into an account at an exchange.

159
00:05:53,941 --> 00:05:55,957
An exchange in Australia asks who

160
00:05:55,957 --> 00:05:57,757
you are before you can deposit.

161
00:05:58,277 --> 00:06:00,580
That check is required by Australian

162
00:06:00,580 --> 00:06:02,512
anti-money laundering law, and

163
00:06:02,512 --> 00:06:04,740
the exchange runs it, not this course.

164
00:06:05,260 --> 00:06:07,264
You only upload identification

165
00:06:07,264 --> 00:06:09,119
inside the exchange's own app.

166
00:06:09,639 --> 00:06:11,706
This course does not name an exchange

167
00:06:11,706 --> 00:06:13,270
and does not show a balance.

168
00:06:13,531 --> 00:06:16,073
Any exchange offering crypto services

169
00:06:16,073 --> 00:06:18,692
in Australia must register with AUSTRAC,

170
00:06:18,692 --> 00:06:21,312
Australia's financial crime regulator,

171
00:06:21,312 --> 00:06:23,623
as a virtual asset service provider.

172
00:06:24,143 --> 00:06:25,407
AUSTRAC keeps a public

173
00:06:25,407 --> 00:06:26,748
register you can search.

174
00:06:27,259 --> 00:06:29,437
Check that register before you sign up.

175
00:06:29,910 --> 00:06:32,201
The register says the business is listed.

176
00:06:32,628 --> 00:06:34,862
It does not say your money is protected.

177
00:06:35,142 --> 00:06:37,737
Registration is not a safety guarantee.

178
00:06:38,257 --> 00:06:40,187
ASIC is Australia's financial

179
00:06:40,187 --> 00:06:41,449
services regulator.

180
00:06:41,969 --> 00:06:44,910
From April 2027, platforms that hold

181
00:06:44,910 --> 00:06:47,754
crypto for you need an ASIC licence,

182
00:06:47,754 --> 00:06:50,695
with a transition period to October

183
00:06:50,695 --> 00:06:53,832
2027 for platforms already operating.

184
00:06:54,352 --> 00:06:56,363
Check ASIC for the current position.

185
00:06:56,610 --> 00:06:58,591
This lesson names only government

186
00:06:58,591 --> 00:07:00,043
bodies, never an exchange.

187
00:07:01,075 --> 00:07:03,312
A second code, as well as your password,

188
00:07:03,312 --> 00:07:05,189
is two-factor authentication.

189
00:07:05,709 --> 00:07:07,809
Turn that on before any real dollars.

190
00:07:08,329 --> 00:07:10,363
Then look for a paper or demo mode.

191
00:07:10,949 --> 00:07:12,470
Paper uses pretend money.

192
00:07:12,990 --> 00:07:14,611
That practice is lesson 7.

193
00:07:15,661 --> 00:07:17,169
A fill is when your waiting

194
00:07:17,169 --> 00:07:18,456
order actually trades.

195
00:07:18,976 --> 00:07:21,267
A maker fee is the charge when your order

196
00:07:21,267 --> 00:07:23,389
waits and someone else trades with it.

197
00:07:23,599 --> 00:07:25,275
A taker fee is the charge when

198
00:07:25,275 --> 00:07:27,007
your order fills straight away.

199
00:07:27,344 --> 00:07:29,042
A grid uses waiting orders,

200
00:07:29,042 --> 00:07:30,741
so read the maker fee first.

201
00:07:31,261 --> 00:07:32,713
Some bots also pay a taker

202
00:07:32,713 --> 00:07:34,166
fee when they first start.

203
00:07:34,574 --> 00:07:37,397
In this example both are 0.1 percent.

204
00:07:37,917 --> 00:07:40,997
On an example buy of 100 Australian

205
00:07:40,997 --> 00:07:43,758
dollars, 0.1 percent is 10 cents.

206
00:07:44,278 --> 00:07:46,401
The sell costs about 10 cents as well.

207
00:07:46,722 --> 00:07:48,566
The round trip is about 20

208
00:07:48,566 --> 00:07:50,498
cents, which is 0.2 percent.

209
00:07:51,018 --> 00:07:53,056
This fee row is Australian dollars.

210
00:07:53,576 --> 00:07:54,638
It is not the July

211
00:07:54,638 --> 00:07:56,308
to October example window.

212
00:07:56,828 --> 00:07:59,139
If the gap is smaller than that 0.2

213
00:07:59,139 --> 00:08:01,616
percent, each completed buy and sell

214
00:08:01,616 --> 00:08:04,257
costs more in fees than the gap is worth.

215
00:08:04,777 --> 00:08:06,732
To compare them, turn the gap into

216
00:08:06,732 --> 00:08:08,904
a percent by dividing it by the price.

217
00:08:09,424 --> 00:08:12,421
In the lesson 1 example, 900

218
00:08:12,421 --> 00:08:15,827
divided by 27,630 is about 3.3

219
00:08:15,827 --> 00:08:19,369
percent, well above 0.2 percent.

220
00:08:19,889 --> 00:08:21,433
Lesson 5 is where you test

221
00:08:21,433 --> 00:08:22,757
a gap against this fee.

222
00:08:23,277 --> 00:08:25,344
You do not pick a gap in this lesson.

223
00:08:25,977 --> 00:08:27,863
A stablecoin is a coin built

224
00:08:27,863 --> 00:08:30,077
to stay worth about one US dollar.

225
00:08:30,597 --> 00:08:32,217
It can still lose that value.

226
00:08:32,685 --> 00:08:35,139
If your pair is priced in a stablecoin,

227
00:08:35,139 --> 00:08:37,671
every fill trades one coin for another,

228
00:08:37,671 --> 00:08:39,096
so every fill is a swap.

229
00:08:40,146 --> 00:08:42,351
A trading journal is a dated record

230
00:08:42,351 --> 00:08:43,644
of every bot: the plan,

231
00:08:43,644 --> 00:08:45,545
the changes, the fills, the fees,

232
00:08:45,545 --> 00:08:47,446
a review note of what happened,

233
00:08:47,446 --> 00:08:49,651
and whether you followed the rules.

234
00:08:50,171 --> 00:08:51,959
That note is not money you made.

235
00:08:52,308 --> 00:08:54,442
A stablecoin fill goes in the journal.

236
00:08:54,962 --> 00:08:57,274
The tax office treats a swap like a sale,

237
00:08:57,274 --> 00:08:59,225
so every swap goes in the journal.

238
00:08:59,745 --> 00:09:02,030
The journal is also the page you will

239
00:09:02,030 --> 00:09:04,239
open again in lesson 8 and lesson 10.

240
00:09:04,759 --> 00:09:07,284
For every transaction, including a buy,

241
00:09:07,284 --> 00:09:09,336
a sell, a swap, or a transfer, write

242
00:09:09,336 --> 00:09:11,783
the date and time, the amount, the value

243
00:09:11,783 --> 00:09:13,993
in Australian dollars at the time,

244
00:09:13,993 --> 00:09:16,439
the fee, which exchange or wallet, what

245
00:09:16,439 --> 00:09:18,728
it was for, and who the other side was.

246
00:09:19,248 --> 00:09:21,322
The columns are the date, the pair,

247
00:09:21,322 --> 00:09:23,089
the range, the gaps, the money

248
00:09:23,089 --> 00:09:24,548
in the bot, the stop rule,

249
00:09:24,548 --> 00:09:26,699
the fills, the fees, the Australian

250
00:09:26,699 --> 00:09:28,850
dollar value of each fill, a review

251
00:09:28,850 --> 00:09:30,861
note, and a yes or no for the rules.

252
00:09:31,367 --> 00:09:33,004
The Australian dollar value

253
00:09:33,004 --> 00:09:34,573
column is the one exception.

254
00:09:35,093 --> 00:09:36,602
It is for your tax records.

255
00:09:37,119 --> 00:09:39,222
Download your exchange's transaction

256
00:09:39,222 --> 00:09:41,233
history at least every three months.

257
00:09:41,654 --> 00:09:42,660
Keep those records

258
00:09:42,660 --> 00:09:44,001
for at least five years.

259
00:09:44,357 --> 00:09:46,129
This is general information,

260
00:09:46,129 --> 00:09:47,015
not tax advice.

261
00:09:47,535 --> 00:09:49,323
Leave two columns blank for now.

262
00:09:49,742 --> 00:09:50,905
One is for the fees.

263
00:09:51,425 --> 00:09:53,716
One is for whether you followed the rule.

264
00:09:53,850 --> 00:09:55,023
You fill those later.

265
00:09:55,472 --> 00:09:57,316
You do not fill them with a hope.

266
00:09:57,977 --> 00:10:00,367
Nobody from this course will ask

267
00:10:00,367 --> 00:10:02,315
for a seed phrase, a private

268
00:10:02,315 --> 00:10:03,731
key, or an API secret.

269
00:10:04,251 --> 00:10:06,227
If a message rushes you to send

270
00:10:06,227 --> 00:10:07,650
crypto, or asks for one

271
00:10:07,650 --> 00:10:09,310
of those, treat it as a scam.

272
00:10:09,830 --> 00:10:11,059
It is not this course.

273
00:10:12,071 --> 00:10:14,566
ASIC's Moneysmart website is the public

274
00:10:14,566 --> 00:10:16,767
Australian guide for scam warnings.

275
00:10:17,287 --> 00:10:18,293
Check it yourself.

276
00:10:19,283 --> 00:10:21,587
Now cover the chart and say your page

277
00:10:21,587 --> 00:10:23,276
out loud: the pair, the range,

278
00:10:23,276 --> 00:10:24,889
the gaps and the stop rule.

279
00:10:25,409 --> 00:10:27,410
Name the pair, the range, the gaps,

280
00:10:27,410 --> 00:10:29,103
and the stop rule, then stop.

281
00:10:29,623 --> 00:10:31,743
If a word on it has not been defined,

282
00:10:31,743 --> 00:10:33,863
stop and define it before you go on.

283
00:10:34,383 --> 00:10:36,488
A secret is a seed phrase,

284
00:10:36,488 --> 00:10:39,015
a private key, or an API secret.

285
00:10:39,535 --> 00:10:41,211
None of those go on this page.

286
00:10:41,609 --> 00:10:43,846
You write Example on your own notebook

287
00:10:43,846 --> 00:10:45,874
so a practice sketch is never later

288
00:10:45,874 --> 00:10:47,272
mistaken for a live plan.

289
00:10:48,322 --> 00:10:50,702
Recap: your page has seven items, each

290
00:10:50,702 --> 00:10:52,843
with a written fact, every figure

291
00:10:52,843 --> 00:10:55,144
marked Example, and no secrets on it.

292
00:10:56,344 --> 00:10:57,758
Next lesson: How a grid

293
00:10:57,758 --> 00:10:59,329
bot works, order by order.

294
00:10:59,849 --> 00:11:01,469
Bring your notes: your range,

295
00:11:01,469 --> 00:11:03,089
your gaps and your stop rule.

296
00:11:03,331 --> 00:11:04,553
Keep the word Example

297
00:11:04,553 --> 00:11:05,979
on every practice figure.

298
00:11:06,499 --> 00:11:08,147
Next lesson you will see what

299
00:11:08,147 --> 00:11:09,767
